               Summary of ARUNZ Parameters as of Version 0.9U

                                  Jay Sage
                 еееееееее DOC file.  It is
separated from the alias_name_form by any number of tabs and spaces.  Thus the
scripts can be formatted neatеееееееее A has a summary of the parameters and directives with no
explanation.  Sections B through D explain the parameters in greater еееееееееnected by equal signs.  There must be no imbedded spaces or
tabs.  An individual alias name expression can be of any length.  Iеееееееееl parameter expressions
supported by ARUNZ version 0.9U.  Finally, Section D covers the special
directives recognized by ARUNZеееееееееame may contain several special
symbols which qualify comparisons with the command verb passed to ARUNZ. 
These are as followsеееееееее--

format	'f'	=	H   hex			D   3 decimal
			F   floating decimal	T   two decimal
			A   ASCII		O   one decimal

module	'm'еееееееееands LDIR,
		XDIR, and so on.  There must be a character present.

comma (,)	Characters that appear after this character are еееееееее   XFCB
			L   MCL

component  'c'	=	N   FN			D   drive or default
			T   FT			U   user or default
			F   FN[.FT]		P   prefеееееееееmple,
		"ZP,ATCH" will respond to ZP or ZPATCH but not to ZPTCH.

greater (>)	This special character represents any name follеееееееее			H  hour (mil)
			M  month		C  hour (civ)
			D  day			N  minute
						A  AM/PM


parameter forms:	full token		$#
			parеееееееееprocessor.
		An example alias definition would be

			>Z80  z80asm $tn0

		Just entering the name with its file type of Z80еееееееее	memory contents		$Mf####
			register contents	$Rf##
			system file		$Sc#
			get prompted input	$"prompt"
			use prompted inеееееееее	any command and is thus the default alias definition.  If used
		at all, it should be the last alias defined.  A common use isеееееееееt	$@f+m####
			date/time		$Du

=============================================================================

B. Definitionеееееееее parameters use qualifiers to further define the data returned 
by that parameter.  Since a number of parameters share some of еееееееее script is the prototype command line.  It contains normal characters
and parameter expressions, as discussed in detail in thisеееееееее               October 2, 1990


     The information about the ARUNZ parameters is covered in four sections
below.  Sectionеееееееееly to make the file easier to read.

     The alias_name_form consists of any number of individual alias name
expressions conеееееееееdetail. 
Section B defines some qualifier symbols that are used with a number of
parameter forms.  Section C defines the actuaеееееееееt is not
limited to the eight characters of file names, and it can contain control
characters, unlike file names.  The alias nеееееееее.


=============================================================================

A. Parameter Summary
------------------еееееееее:

underscore (_)	This character in the alias name will match any character.
		Thus the alias name "_DIR" will match the commеееееееее	=	B   BIOS		M   MSG
			C   CCP/CPR		N   NDR
			D   DOS			P   PATH
			E   ENV			R   RCP
			F   FCP			S   SHL
			I   IOP			Xеееееееееoptional.
		If characters are present in the command, they must match.
		However, the characters need not be present.  For exaеееееееееix (with colon)
			Z   Crunched Type	S   short prefix
			Q   Squeezed Type	C   converted token (to DU:)

units	'u'	=	Y  yearеееееееееowed by a
		period.  It is used with ZCPR34, which can accept a command
		like "PROG.Z80" and pass it to the extended command еееееееееsed token		$Tc#
			home directory		$HD  $HU  $HB
			full command line	$!
			full command tail	$*
			tail less tokens	$-#
		еееееееее would invoke
		the Z80ASM assembler on the file name.

colon (:)	An alias name that begins with this character will match
	еееееееееput	$'c#  ( c: "L" ";" "E" )
			ENV address		$Am
			offset ENV address	$+m
			ENV address content	$@fAm
			offset ENV contenеееееееее
		to make ARUNZ chain to LX or another extended command
		processor.  For example:

			:DEFAULT LX / $!
    
     Severalеееееееееs
--------------

     A line in the ALIAS.CMD file takes the following form:

	alias_name_form   alias_script

The aliasеееееееееthese 
qualifiers, we would like to list them here at the beginning.

1. Conversion formats (f):

     When byte values areеееееееееted file specification (DIR: form, if
			present, changed to equivalent DU: -- if invalid
			name, then no conversion is perfoеееееееееin the parameter expression as follows.

	 format qualifier		meaning
	 ----------------		-------

		A		ASCII, uses the valuеееееееее------------

1. Command line token parameters: $#

     The parameter expression $#, where '#' is a number between 0 and 9,еееееееее three decimal characters
		T		return value as two decimal characters
		O		return value as one decimal character

2. System еееееееееmmand line, including any
directory prefix and/or file type.  Parameters numbered from 1 to 9 return
the corresponding token fеееееееееis specified by one of the following
letters:

	 module qualifier		memory module
	 ----------------		-------------
		B			BIеееееееее
spaces as delimiters between tokens, but it can be configured to allow tabs
as delimiters as well.


2. Command line token еееееееее
		F			FCP	(flow command package)
		I			IOP	(input/output package)
		L			MCL	(multiple command line)
		M			MSG	(message buffeеееееееее parsed as file specification expressions using
the parameter form $Tc#, where 'c' is the component part of the file
specificaеееееееееll stack)
		X			XFCB	(external command FCB)


3. File specification components (c):

     Several parameters return variouеееееееееb; the values
1..9 refer to the corresponding token in the command line tail.

     Under ZCPR33 and earlier, if the command еееееееееree letters can be used with all such 
parameters. The remaining letters can be used with those parameters that 
refer to tokeеееееееее.  Thus $TT0, the file type of the command
verb, would always be null.  The expressions $TD0 and $TU0 would always
return the еееееееееname)
